  "summary": "Ph.D. | Cross-layer software architect and hands-on developer\n\nI design systems and I build them. At Sonatus, I'm the software architect for the Cloud AI team on a software-defined vehicle platform, while still owning features end to end, from requirements through design and implementation to deployment.\n\nWhat I work on now\n- AI agents: turning demo-level, prompt-based agents into deterministic, production-ready systems using workflows, state machines, and persistent domain models. I designed the Log-View-Intention (LVI) model for auditable, resumable agent execution.\n- Server-side and data: vehicle configuration and state management, a generic policy engine, OPA-based access control, and device-to-cloud data pipelines.\n- Engineering foundation: coding standards, implementation templates, shared libraries, and release processes that keep teams building one way. I also maintain our internal Claude Code plugin marketplace, with skills, agents, and harnesses for our development process, which several other teams have since adopted as the pattern for their own.\n\nHow I design\n- Seek appropriate design rather than over-engineering, just open enough for future change.\n- Don't reinvent the wheel. Use existing design theories, patterns, libraries, and frameworks.\n- Revisit established concepts in new domains. Innovation often comes from there.\n\nHow I got here\nMy career has moved across the whole stack: storage firmware and OS research during my Ph.D., ARM/KVM virtualization and a smart home IoT edge framework at Samsung, a spatial-AI retail service, and now cloud backend and AI agents for connected vehicles. The common thread is connected devices and the systems around them.\n\nStack: Go · Python · Kubernetes · GraphQL Federation · Kafka · PostgreSQL · LangGraph · MCP\nPreviously: Kotlin · Flutter · C/C++ · Linux kernel · KVM",
